{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Uncover blind spots in AWS data plane operations with CloudTrail Event Coverage - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail's new feature, Event Coverage, allows users to view their data plane operations coverage at the account and organization levels in the AWS CloudTrail console. This feature helps identify gaps in logging posture by showing which AWS services and resource types have data event logging enabled and which do not. Data events enable tracking of data plane operations in AWS services, such as Amazon S3 object-level operations, to detect unauthorized access or exfiltration attempts. Event Coverage provides a centralized view of coverage across all supported data event sources and enables subscribing to data events directly from the dashboard. This simplifies closing coverage gaps, especially for organizations managing multiple accounts. Event Coverage is available in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS CloudTrail is supported. For more information on logging Data Events, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/aws-cloudtrail-event-coverage/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS CloudTrail adds data event aggregation to simplify security monitoring - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for AWS account activity, introduces a new feature called CloudTrail aggregated events. This feature simplifies monitoring and analysis of high-volume CloudTrail data events by consolidating them into 5-minute summaries. Security, compliance, and operations teams can efficiently identify trends like access frequency, error rates, and frequently used actions without processing individual events. Aggregations are available for API activity, resource access, and user activity summaries. Users can enable aggregation through the AWS console or CLI and choose from pre-built templates. The aggregation service is charged based on the number of CloudTrail data events analyzed. It is available in all commercial AWS Regions. With this feature, teams can answer questions like \"How has this user's activity changed over the past week?\" or \"What are the top actions being performed on this critical resource?\" without having to scan through massive amounts of CloudTrail data events","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-data-event-aggregation-security-monitoring/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"AWS Private CA now provides detailed certificate issuance logs - AWS","context":"AWS Private CA has introduced detailed certificate issuance logs as a new AWS CloudTrail service event. This feature records the complete certificate content, CA information, requester identity, and signing status for every certificate issuance. The new IssueCertificateDetails event captures the TBS certificate with all X.509 fields and extensions, subject, issuer, serial number, validity period, template, signing algorithm, and failure explanations for pre-signing failures. The events are delivered automatically as CloudTrail management events, with no additional cost or configuration required. They can be used for compliance auditing, certificate inventory, algorithm migration tracking, and failed-issuance monitoring. The events identify the requester, and are delivered to the CA owner account in cross-account configurations. This feature is available in all AWS Regions where AWS Private CA is offered","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-private-ca-certificate-issuance-logs/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Analyze your CloudTrail events using natural language in Amazon Q Console - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service used for recording API activity across AWS accounts for security auditing, compliance, and operational troubleshooting, now integrates with Amazon Q Console. This integration enables users to investigate AWS account activity using natural language. Users can ask Amazon Q Console about CloudTrail configuration, query logged events for security investigations, and troubleshoot operational issues without writing queries or manually parsing log files. With this integration, users can check if CloudTrail trails are properly configured, identify gaps in logging coverage, and confirm which data event sources are being tracked. For security concerns, users can ask about who accessed specific IAM roles, changes made to VPC configurations, and unauthorized access attempts. For operational troubleshooting, users can ask about who created or deleted resources, identify API calls generating errors, trace activity from specific IP addresses, or determine why bills spiked. Amazon Q Console can query CloudTrail trails, associated CloudWatch log groups, and event data stores on behalf of users, providing answers grounded in actual account activity. This integration is available in all AWS commercial regions where Amazon Q Console is supported. To get started, users can open Amazon Q in the AWS Management Console and ask questions about their CloudTrail configuration or account activity. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/cloudtrail-amazon-q-console/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify CloudTrail org trail GetEventSelectors behavior from member accounts in docs."}

